# 孫子 (Sun Tzu) — where lessons live and how they reach each agent

## Answer
File each lesson once in a private, user-global store (`~/.agents/lessons/`), one file per lesson with a `scope` (all agents or one engine) and a `trigger` (the paths, commands or task kinds it bites on), and keep each project's facts in a committed, path-keyed file at that project's root. Deliver both mechanically, never by asking an agent to remember: the guard hook pushes the matching lesson into the main agent's context at the moment it writes a matching path, and the brief writer copies the matching rule line into every engine's brief. If EJ wants the lessons public, the same shape moves into this repo and the delivery does not change.

## How I decided
1. I counted what each reader can actually reach before choosing a store (my #1: 「夫未戰而廟算勝者，得算多也；未戰而廟算不勝者，得算少也。多算勝，少算不勝，而況於無算乎？」). Facts: the main agent's lessons sit in one project's Claude Code memory; an engine reads its brief or its working repo; opencode refuses outside-repo paths and agy soft-denies shell and web; the guard is installed once in `~/.claude/settings.json` and roots at whatever git repo it is called in (`guard/src` SPEC l.22); a skill loads only when its description matches. My reading: no single file is read by all three, so the store's location decides nothing by itself — the gate that pushes it decides.
2. So I chose the gate over the reminder (my #12: 「故善戰者，求之於勢，不責於人，故能擇人而任勢。」). Change the condition under which the lesson appears — at the write, and inside the brief — instead of exhorting the agent to read. My #2 gives the split: 「故用兵之法，無恃其不來，恃吾有以待之；無恃其不攻，恃吾有所不可攻也。」 — build the side you own (the gate); do not rely on the agent's remembering.
3. The gate must read the store from any project, so the store is user-global: `~/.agents/lessons/`, a private git repo, one Markdown file per lesson. Not option (b) — a skill in this repo: a skill loads only on a description match, and this repo is public, so a store that names other projects' traps would be published.
4. Do not route delivery through "go read the store" (my #13: 「途有所不由，軍有所不擊，城有所不攻，地有所不爭，君命有所不受。」). A command an engine will not accept is not delivery: opencode and agy cannot read outside their repo, so for them the line must be in the brief text.
5. One type vs all agents: a `scope:` field in the lesson file, one directory, no per-engine folders (my #3: 「是故百戰百勝，非善之善者也；不戰而屈人之兵，善之善者也。」 — one copy that stays whole beats copies per engine). Scope decides who it is pushed to, not who may read it: the main agent may read every scope, because it must know an engine's limits to brief it.
6. Project facts are keyed by path, not by a folder. My reading of the three options: a comment at the line is read only when the line is read and disappears in a refactor; a file in the code's folder drifts and is invisible from the root; the code map is designed, not built, so it can hold nothing today. What is left is one committed file at the repo root, `.agents/lessons.yaml`, mapping a path glob to one line. "Kept with the code" then means reachable from the path in one mechanical hop (my #10: avoid the solid, strike the empty — put the fact where the write is empty of it, not in a general doc).
7. Every lesson must name its ground (my #19: 「九地之變，屈伸之利，人情之理，不可不察也。」). `triggers:` is required — path globs, command patterns, task kinds. A lesson with no trigger is not filed yet (my #5: 「非利不動」... wait, exact quote is 「故曰：明主慮之，良將修之，非利不動，非得不用，非危不戰。」 — use just 「非利不動」? That's an exact substring, fine. Or cite without quote.)
